### [pluiedev](/pluiedev) [Aug 3, 2025](#discussioncomment-13975948) Collaborator

| Just to avoid appearing as if we're ignoring this thread — the whole rationale behind the W3C input rework is that keybinds would be based on the correct keys on the actual keyboard, since we have had a lot of bugs in the past where certain keybinds were not possible on other keyboard layouts due to how their keys were mapped.<br>The reason this hasn't been turned into an issue is because it's not clear how XKB remaps should factor into this: `caps:swapescape` isn't unique; all XKB remap rules are currently ignored under the rework. The problem is that if we do apply those remap rules we directly go against *why* the rework was necessary in the first place — to avoid any potential bugs with different key remapping logic in different layouts. It could be argued that in this case the user specifically *wants* the Caps Lock key to be indistinguishable from the Escape key in all pieces of software, but then we would be at the mercy of XKB to correctly interpret those rules.<br>There's no straightforward answer to this that's general enough to cover all use cases. As far as I know this problem doesn't really exist on macOS or Windows or the web (for which the W3C spec was originally written), since you can't easily remap keys there without some external tool that explicitly emulates keypresses. For `caps:swapescape` specifically I can see us conceding and adding a config option just for it, but I'm not so sure about whether this would be ideal for all settings, since testing different layouts and configurations is *already* nightmarish as is. I'm personally open to different opinions though. |
