#### [aspiers](/aspiers) [Aug 3, 2025](#discussioncomment-13978136)

- I agree with [@ncfavier](https://github.com/ncfavier) here.Addendum: I guess the core philosophical issue is: *what* we should accept as the "real" source of physical keys — the key events themselves or a virtualized interpretation processed by XKB? I'm not an expert on this topic so again I consider this an open question.I could be missing something, but speaking from the experience of [delving quite deep into XKB](https://github.com/aspiers/desktop-config/tree/master/.xkb), I don't see any way ghostty can currently function correctly on Linux without honoring XKB mappings.I presume the W3C input rework you refer to is [#7320](https://github.com/ghostty-org/ghostty/pull/7320). I have to admit I'm confused why it makes sense to base a terminal app's approach to key handling on a standard designed for the web, and it's not entirely clear to me from that PR, but maybe it would become more obvious if I read the entire spec.I also noticed that 3 of the 4 bugs fixed by [#7320](https://github.com/ghostty-org/ghostty/pull/7320) are MacOS bugs, and the [4th is reported to have been fixed on MacOS but still broken on Linux](https://github.com/ghostty-org/ghostty/discussions/6724#discussioncomment-12771772). To add to my confusion, I don't think MacOS uses XKB by default (although it appears there are non-Apple hacks for that), so whatever benefit [#7320](https://github.com/ghostty-org/ghostty/pull/7320) accomplished for MacOS seems to be negatively impacting Linux.You can do a *lot* of weird things with XKB and I personally think it's a bit too advanced for just a keyboard layout manager, though. It's more akin to Karabiner Elements in terms of functionality, but prebuilt into the system.I definitely agree XKB is complex and hard to understand. However I don't think these personal opinions can justify stopping respecting XKB remappings. Like it or not, it's the de facto standard on Linux distros which almost all other apps respect (with a few exceptions in the Javascript / Electron world which in my experience are typically accidental and/or due to a lack of awareness of the difference between physical and logical key events). So from a practical perspective, not respecting XKB mappings on Linux seem doomed to create endless problems.Unfortunately it makes ghostty totally unusable in my particular case (see [#8130](https://github.com/ghostty-org/ghostty/discussions/8130)), which also shows that ghostty is only *partially* ignoring XKB mappings:

1. It *does* respect that I've bound the Delete key to a modifier (Alt/Meta)
2. It *doesn't* respect that I've removed the `Delete` keysym binding from the key.
The end result is that pressing the key is treated as `Alt+Delete`, which is the worst of both worlds. At very least ghostty should be consistent with itself. But as noted above, I'm struggling to understand how it can ever work reliably for Linux users without respecting XKB. Perhaps this was an unintentional side-effect of [#7320](https://github.com/ghostty-org/ghostty/pull/7320), but IMHO it definitely needs to be promoted to an issue if a goal of ghostty is to continue supporting Linux users.

