### [pluiedev](/pluiedev) [Aug 21, 2025](#discussioncomment-14179938) Collaborator

| After looking at this a *bit* more I think there might be a compromise to be had here.<br>The vast majority of our input bugs in 1.1.3 were caused by keys that emit printable Unicode codepoints, i.e. `Binding.Trigger.Key.unicode` keys, and we defaulted to using the *translated* keys instead of physical keys, which caused a lot of *impossible* binds where certain translated keys require modifiers that cannot be input on a normal keyboard (e.g. `Shift``+` requiring `Shift``Shift``=` to trigger).<br>After the W3C input rewrite, this worked *great* for those printable keys, but we were left with a conundrum for *special keys* like `Esc` and `Caps Lock`, which are only ever physical keys and never Unicode keys since, well, they don't produce a Unicode codepoint. And as evident in this thread, it's desirable for Ghostty to run those special keys through the remapper so that XKB remaps still work as intended.<br>I'm going to try to PR this as a potential solution. I think this is the best of both worlds as we won't get impossible binds like `Shift``+` anymore, while keeping `caps:swapescape` and the like functional. There might be some edge cases here and there, but the community response I think has been overwhelmingly supportive of us moving in this direction. |
| --- |

